<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
PrivacyFence *companion* autostart Task Scheduler task definition.

scripts/windows_privilege_separation.ps1 substitutes __EXEC_PATH__ with the
real installed PrivacyFenceCompanion.exe path and registers it via
`schtasks /create /tn "PrivacyFenceCompanion" /xml <file> /f`, from its
`enable`, which the installer runs on every install. It is the only
Scheduled Task a PrivacyFence install has.

This is ADR 0002's "startup wiring inverts" on Windows. What used to start
at sign-in was the daemon, from a task of its own that the installer
registered and `enable` then disabled; that task is gone (ADR 0042's
cleanup), and the daemon is a service running as NT SERVICE\PrivacyFence.
On this platform the companion is not merely a convenience: a service runs
in session 0, so the daemon cannot call webbrowser.open() for a
Slack/Salesforce/Atlassian OAuth flow, and the companion's own control
channel is what opens those pages instead (ADR 0002 decision 5).

The structure follows Microsoft's own "any group member" logon trigger
example, and several details of it are load bearing; the daemon task this
replaced shipped without each of them at some point, and every time it
registered without complaint and then did not work:

  * version="1.2" on the root element. MultipleInstancesPolicy and
    RestartOnFailure are schema 1.2 constructs; a definition that does not
    declare the version is validated against 1.0, where they do not exist.
  * id on Principal and the matching Context on Actions. That id reference
    is what binds the action set to the security context it runs under;
    without it the GroupId below is bound to nothing that runs.
  * StartBoundary on the trigger, the point from which it is live at all.
  * DisallowStartIfOnBatteries and StopIfGoingOnBatteries set to false. Both
    default to true, which on a laptop means "do not start at sign in on
    battery" and "stop when unplugged". Only noticed once a test read the
    definition back out of Task Scheduler (schtasks /query /xml).
  * No encoding attribute on the XML declaration. schtasks hands the file to
    MSXML as a Unicode stream, and a declaration that contradicts the stream
    fails with "(1,40)::ERROR: unable to switch the encoding". The content is
    pure ASCII, so every reading of it agrees.

Two things differ from the daemon task, and each one is deliberate:

  * No TimeTrigger. The daemon task carried a repeating PT5M trigger as its
    crash-restart mechanism and relied on the daemon's single-instance lock
    to make every redundant tick exit at once. The companion has no such
    lock, so a repeating trigger would stack up one tray icon per tick.
    Crash restart for the daemon is the service's own `sc failure`
    configuration; for the companion there is no automatic restart, and the
    Start Menu shortcut the installer creates is the manual one.

  * MultipleInstancesPolicy is IgnoreNew, not Parallel. Same reason: a
    second tray icon for the same session is never wanted. It stays correct
    for multiple simultaneously signed-in users, because Task Scheduler
    scopes that policy per task instance and each user's logon produces its
    own.

RestartOnFailure is not a keep-alive (measured: Task Scheduler logs a
killed action as a successfully completed task, so it never engages for an
action that ran and then died). It stays for the one narrow thing it does:
a launch failure right at the LogonTrigger's own moment (a locked file
mid-upgrade) is retried three times a minute apart rather than leaving the
user with no way into the web UI until their next sign-in.

LogonTrigger with no UserId fires for any interactive logon, and Principal
uses GroupId (the built-in Users group) rather than UserId, so the companion
runs as whichever user just signed in, in their own session, which is the
whole point of it. It must be LeastPrivilege: this process is deliberately
on the *agent's* side of the trust boundary, not the daemon's.

NOTE: this comment intentionally avoids the two-hyphen sequence, which XML
comments may not contain anywhere in their body.
-->
<Task version="1.2" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/windows/2004/02/mit/task">
  <RegistrationInfo>
    <Description>Starts the PrivacyFence companion (tray icon and browser-opening channel) when any user signs in.</Description>
  </RegistrationInfo>
  <Triggers>
    <LogonTrigger>
      <StartBoundary>2020-01-01T00:00:00</StartBoundary>
      <Enabled>true</Enabled>
    </LogonTrigger>
  </Triggers>
  <Principals>
    <Principal id="Author">
      <GroupId>Builtin\Users</GroupId>
      <RunLevel>LeastPrivilege</RunLevel>
    </Principal>
  </Principals>
  <Settings>
    <MultipleInstancesPolicy>IgnoreNew</MultipleInstancesPolicy>
    <Enabled>true</Enabled>
    <DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>false</DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>
    <StopIfGoingOnBatteries>false</StopIfGoingOnBatteries>
    <AllowStartOnDemand>true</AllowStartOnDemand>
    <AllowHardTerminate>true</AllowHardTerminate>
    <RestartOnFailure>
      <Interval>PT1M</Interval>
      <Count>3</Count>
    </RestartOnFailure>
  </Settings>
  <Actions Context="Author">
    <Exec>
      <Command>__EXEC_PATH__</Command>
    </Exec>
  </Actions>
</Task>
